{"availability":"available","full":"chochain/weForth","name":"weForth","description":"Web eForth via WASM","category":"Other","language":"C++","stars":10,"forks":4,"created":"2022-11-19T02:45:48Z","updated":"2026-06-17T01:08:44Z","branch":"master","homepage":"","topics":["forth","wasm"],"demo":null,"color":"#63362a","ink":"#f8c7a8","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-04T06:52:01.115Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-04T06:52:01.115Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-04T06:52:01.115Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-03T21:19:19.236Z","stars":10,"forks":4},{"at":"2026-10-04T06:52:01.115Z","stars":10,"forks":4}],"technologies":[],"lastDemoCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T09:39:11.499Z","overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["WebAssembly enpowered eForth on web browsers. Is it faster? Is it more portable? Yes, and Yes.","Well, on my aged laptop, the impression is pretty exciting! It's at least 5x faster than pure Javascript implementation on a browser and at 60% speed of C/C++ natively compiled code on CPU. It was at 25% of native a year ago but as Javascript JIT improves, it now runs faster as well. Not bad at all! On the portability end, though not exactly plug-and-play but some simple alteration turned my C code web-enabled. Of course, WASM has yet to integrate with the front-end well enough, so updating DOM is a different feat. If we want to venture beyond being a terminal app some UI glue is still…","Regardless, it brought me warm smiles seeing eForth run in a browser. Better yet, it's straight from C/C++ source code. Other popular scripting languages such as Python, Ruby are trending toward WASM/WASI implementation as well. However, unlike FORTH, they depend mostly on JIT without a built-in compiler, the interpreter-in-an-interpreter design will likely cap the top-end performance (i.e. stuck at 1015% of native speed, so far)."],"features":["supports 32-bit float","call interface from FORTH into Javascript functions","integrated with Web graphics (SDL2, WebGL) and Physics Engine (Jolt)","built-in editor, IDE-style interactive front-end","access to ss, dict, and VM memory (via WebAssembly.Memory) from Javascript"],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/chochain/weForth/blob/master/README.md","fingerprint":"0c52d077313aef66a033f5f3ca67a2414c7dfeb622af41e2f0bbe6e48e3e1cc2","extractedAt":"2026-10-03T06:15:22.463Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T09:39:11.497Z","overviewRepoUpdated":"2026-06-17T01:08:44Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"WebAssembly enpowered eForth on web browsers. Is it faster? Is it more portable? Yes, and Yes.","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/chochain/weForth/blob/master/README.md"},"audience":[],"requirements":[{"kind":"hosting","status":"documented","text":"browser can fetch from self-host web server (i.g. python3 -m http.server)","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/chochain/weForth/blob/master/README.md"}],"fingerprint":"0c52d077313aef66a033f5f3ca67a2414c7dfeb622af41e2f0bbe6e48e3e1cc2","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/chochain/weForth/blob/master/README.md","checkedAt":"2026-10-03T09:39:11.498Z"},"readmeSnapshot":{"version":1,"status":"saved","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/chochain/weForth/blob/master/README.md","checkedAt":"2026-10-03T09:39:11.498Z","hash":"0c52d077313aef66a033f5f3ca67a2414c7dfeb622af41e2f0bbe6e48e3e1cc2","originalBytes":9098,"storedBytes":9098,"truncated":false,"searchTerms":["weforth","forth","wasm","jolt","web","engine","faster","javascript","review","worker","break","case","emscripten","physics","sdl","file","make","build","ceforth.html","demo","different","e.data","eforth","graphic","implementation","included","now","runs","sdl2","simple","weforth.html","about","browser","c++","call","code","editor","eforth.html","front-end","functions","generator","interface","link","native","python3","run","speed","system","tests","they","thread","three.js","webgl","aes","aged","become","between","bitbybit","built-in","cascadestudio","cipher","class","clock","color","compiled","daz","debug","dict","does","firefox"